:1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"Strengths: The answer correctly says seed capital is fund assets rather than an expense, noting it is exposed to market risk and may be redeemed. It lists plausible one-time costs (legal formation/registration, listing, index licensing, platform onboarding, distribution setup) and recurring costs (administration, custody, transfer agency, index, listing, audit, legal/compliance). It says that under a unitary fee the sponsor covers specified operating expenses, with contractual exclusions such as brokerage, taxes and extraordinary items, and that investors bear spreads. It also avoids inventing a vendor quote or a dollar range.\\n\\nSubstantive omissions and errors: (1) The break-even explanation is underspecified. It says only that fee revenue (rate × average net assets) must cover sponsor-borne costs. It does not separate fixed from asset-based variable costs, give break-even assets ≈ fixed costs ÷ (retained fee rate − variable cost rate), or state that a solution exists only when that margin is positive. The question explicitly asked for this. (2) It omits the Investment Company Act §14(a) $100,000 net-worth condition. That condition is the regulatory anchor that separates seed requirements from launch spending and break-even assets. (3) Payer allocation for organization costs is not addressed, including whether the manager bears them without recoupment and pays trustees directly, as in Lazard's filing. (4) Listing 'management' as a recurring cost under a unitary fee is muddled, because the unitary fee is the sponsor's revenue. Overall the content is useful, but the central break-even mechanism and key allocation and legal facts are incomplete.\"}","ttft_ms":3911,"type":"result","duration_ms":8776,"uuid":"55e2d818-6ecf-4647-8176-877e878fe38f","ttft_stream_ms":642,"time_to_request_ms":64,"first_content_frame_ms":642,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
